WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.020
A white blood cell is carried along in flowing blood, and it must stop only where

2
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.080
there is an infection.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.460
There, it can stop on the vessel wall in about a second.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.100
What switches its grip on?

5
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.720
A second is too short to make a new protein.

6
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.620
So the receptor that grips must already sit on the cell surface, switched off.

7
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:31.723
The receptor that grips the wall is an integrin.

8
00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:37.863
It is two different chains, called α and β, and each chain crosses the

9
00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:44.083
membrane once. Their large outer parts together form a head that grips its ligand.

10
00:00:44.943 --> 00:00:48.303
Inside the cell, each chain has only a short tail.

11
00:00:48.923 --> 00:00:51.303
There is no kinase domain and no channel.

12
00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:58.137
In integrins studied most closely, those of platelets and white blood cells, the

13
00:00:58.137 --> 00:01:00.217
outer part can fold at a knee.

14
00:01:01.057 --> 00:01:03.677
The head then tips back toward the membrane.

15
00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:09.497
Bent, with the head closed, the integrin holds its ligand weakly.

16
00:01:10.077 --> 00:01:14.317
A white blood cell flowing in the blood keeps its integrins in this form.

17
00:01:14.597 --> 00:01:16.537
So the grip is switched off.

18
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:21.627
A signal on the vessel wall starts a relay inside the cell.

19
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:26.327
The relay helps bring a protein called talin to the integrin tail.

20
00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:31.387
Talin binds the tail of the β chain and separates the two tails.

21
00:01:32.347 --> 00:01:36.747
The change passes through the membrane, the outer part straightens, and the head

22
00:01:36.747 --> 00:01:41.847
opens. Extended like this, the integrin holds its ligand tightly.

23
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.220
The extended integrin grabs its ligand on the vessel wall and holds it tightly.

24
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.620
The cell stops. Nothing had to be made, so the relay can run in about a second.

25
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.840
The message started inside the cell and changed the grip outside.

26
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.100
So this is called inside-out signalling.

27
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:07.283
A hormone receptor waits for its ligand.

28
00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:13.223
An integrin is often surrounded by ligand, so the cell controls the receptor

29
00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:20.023
instead. So ligand can be all around, and the grip stays off until the cell switches

30
00:02:20.023 --> 00:02:25.223
it on. The control is a change of shape, thrown from inside the cell.

31
00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:31.203
Keep this rule: the cell, not the ligand, decides when an integrin grips.
